import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The scans, on JS - the host's own search.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Text.yana` beside this is the tier's documentation and everything both targets share;
   `Text.native.yana` is the vector answer to the same names.

   There is no `Chunked(String)` instance here and there cannot be one: a host string has no
   addressable units, so what a native build reaches through a container this build reaches through
   `String.prototype.indexOf`, `lastIndexOf` and `substring`. Implementation-String.md part 5 rules
   that this is "legitimate, encouraged" rather than a compromise - searching for a fixed run of units
   has no room for the cross-engine disagreement the decoding tier has to worry about, and the
   engine's own C++ beats any `charCodeAt` loop this compiler emits.

   **A host call is taken where the host computes the same function, and nowhere else.** Three of them
   are not taken here and each is worth naming, because the host has all three:

   - `toLowerCase` / `toUpperCase` are Unicode case folding and locale-sensitive. They change unit
     counts and they are not the same function as the native build's `A`-`Z` select, so the ASCII tier
     spells its own loop.
   - `trim` trims the Unicode whitespace set, which is larger than the six units `isSpace` names.
     `Text.yana`'s trim is written over `isSpace` and is one body for both targets for that reason.
   - `startsWith` / `endsWith` return a host boolean, and a `Bool` here is the number 0 or 1
     (`boolNumber` in codegen/js/type.cpp). The `HostCall` arm does not convert, so a predicate taken
     from the host would be right in a condition and wrong in a value position - see
     `Native/Host.js.yana`. `hostSubstring` and `hostStringEq` are the same answer with no such edge:
     one allocation the length of the prefix, and a comparison the engine stops at the first
     difference of.

   `-1` for "not found" is the host's answer and is turned into `Nothing` at each call rather than
   left to leak. The positions are UTF-16 code-unit offsets, which is what `String.unit` and `length`
   mean by a position on this target.
-}

-- One unit as a one-unit string, which is what the host's search takes. `String.fromCharCode` is the
-- only way to build one here, and it is what the whole unit-searching family below is written over.
fn unitText(unit: CodeUnit) -> String = hostFromCharCode(unit :: Int)

pub fn findAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit, from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let at = hostIndexOf(self, unitText(unit), from)
    if at < 0 then return Nothing

    return Just(at)

-- The host search stepped along, which is the same C++ scan restarted at each hit. It is a worse
-- shape than the native mask count and it is still the fastest thing this target has.
pub fn countAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit) -> Int:
    let needle = unitText(unit)
    let &total = 0
    let &from = 0 :: Size

    while from >= 0:
        let at = hostIndexOf(self, needle, from)
        if at < 0 then return total

        total = total + 1
        from = at + 1

    return total

-- `lastIndexOf` takes the position the match may *start* at at the latest, so the whole string is
-- `length(self)` and not `length(self) - 1`.
pub fn findLastAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit) -> Maybe(Size):
    let at = hostLastIndexOf(self, unitText(unit), length(self))
    if at < 0 then return Nothing

    return Just(at)

{-
   Any of a set of units, as the lowest of one host search per candidate.

   The native half does this in one pass with the candidates in registers; there is no host call that
   takes a set, so the choice here is between `k` scans of engine C++ and one `charCodeAt` loop with a
   membership test inside it. The scans win for the candidate counts this is asked about - a delimiter
   set is two or three units - because the engine's scan is a memchr-class loop and the emitted one is
   an interpreted property read per unit. It is `O(k*n)` in the worst case and the loop is `O(n)`, so
   a large set would want the other one; nothing in the library builds one.
-}
pub fn findAsciiAny(self: String, units: [CodeUnit], from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let &best = length(self) + 1
    let &found = False

    for candidate in items(units):
        let at = hostIndexOf(self, unitText(candidate), from)
        if at >= 0 && at < best:
            best = at
            found = True

    if !found then return Nothing

    return Just(best)

-- And the inverse, which has no host call at all: "not one of these" is not a search the engine
-- exposes, so this is the `charCodeAt` walk the family otherwise avoids.
pub fn findAsciiNone(self: String, units: [CodeUnit], from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(self)
    let &i = from
    if i < (0 :: Size) then i = 0
    if length(units) == (0 :: Size) then return if i < count then Just(i) else Nothing

    while i < count:
        if !contains(units, truncate(self.unit(i)) :: CodeUnit) then return Just(i)
        i = i + 1

    return Nothing

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Prefixes, suffixes and whole needles
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `hostSubstring` and `hostStringEq` rather than the host's own `startsWith`, for the reason the
   header gives: a host predicate hands back a host boolean and a `Bool` here is a number. The
   substring is the length of the prefix and the comparison stops at its first differing unit, so what
   this costs against the host predicate is one short allocation.
-}
pub fn startsWith(text: String, prefix: String) -> Bool:
    let wanted = length(prefix)
    if wanted > length(text) then return False

    return hostStringEq(hostSubstring(text, 0, wanted), prefix)

pub fn endsWith(text: String, suffix: String) -> Bool:
    let wanted = length(suffix)
    let count = length(text)
    if wanted > count then return False

    return hostStringEq(hostSubstring(text, count - wanted, count), suffix)

-- An empty needle is found at `from`, which is the same convention the native half states and the
-- same one the host's own `indexOf` has.
pub fn String.find(haystack: String, needle: String, from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(haystack)
    let &begin = from
    if begin < (0 :: Size) then begin = 0
    if begin > count then begin = count
    if length(needle) == (0 :: Size) then return Just(begin)

    let at = hostIndexOf(haystack, needle, begin)
    if at < 0 then return Nothing

    return Just(at)

pub fn String.findLast(haystack: String, needle: String) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(haystack)
    if length(needle) == (0 :: Size) then return Just(count)

    let at = hostLastIndexOf(haystack, needle, count)
    if at < 0 then return Nothing

    return Just(at)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The ASCII case tier, and the ASCII test
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   A `charCodeAt` walk and a `String.fromCharCode` per unit, appended through `String.pushUnit` -
   which is the host's `+` and therefore a rope, so the chain is linear here rather than quadratic.
   The host's own case conversion is deliberately not under these names; see the header.
-}
pub fn toLowerAscii(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        out.pushUnit(toLowerUnit(text.unit(i)))
        i = i + 1

    return out

pub fn toUpperAscii(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        out.pushUnit(toUpperUnit(text.unit(i)))
        i = i + 1

    return out

pub fn equalsAsciiCase(left: String, right: String) -> Bool:
    let count = length(left)
    if count != length(right) then return False

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        if toLowerUnit(left.unit(i)) != toLowerUnit(right.unit(i)) then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

{-
   Case-insensitive ordering - `Text.yana` states the contract, and here it is the walk it reads as.

   There is no container under a host string to load a vector out of, so the loop that this target
   *can* write is the one the native half stopped writing: a unit of each operand per position, with
   the fold applied to both before the comparison. It stops at the first position the two part on,
   which for the strings a case-insensitive comparison is asked about is near the start.
-}
pub fn compareAsciiCase(left: String, right: String) -> Ordering:
    let leftCount = length(left)
    let rightCount = length(right)
    let &shared = leftCount
    if rightCount < shared then shared = rightCount

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < shared:
        let a = toLowerUnit(left.unit(i))
        let b = toLowerUnit(right.unit(i))
        if a < b then return LT
        if a > b then return GT
        i = i + 1

    if leftCount < rightCount then return LT
    if leftCount > rightCount then return GT
    return EQ

pub fn isAllAscii(text: String) -> Bool:
    let count = length(text)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        if !isAscii(text.unit(i)) then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True
